// Copy to Secrets.xcconfig and fill in. Secrets.xcconfig is gitignored.
//
//     cp Secrets.xcconfig.example Secrets.xcconfig
//
// Nothing in Milestone 1 needs any of this. The file exists so that when a real
// recognition provider is added in Phase 2, the credential has an obvious place
// to live that is not the repository.
//
// READ THIS BEFORE USING A REAL KEY
//
// A key embedded in an iOS app is extractable from the app bundle. That is
// acceptable here, and only here, because this is a private prototype installed
// on one phone by the person who owns the key. It stops being acceptable the
// moment the app is given to anyone else or put on TestFlight.
//
// Before that happens, put a small authenticated proxy in front of the provider:
// the app talks to the proxy, the proxy holds the key, and the key can be
// rotated without shipping a new build. Keep the same `RecognitionClient`
// boundary — only the base URL and the auth header change.

RECOGNITION_PROVIDER_BASE_URL = https://example.invalid/v1
RECOGNITION_API_KEY =

// Sent with every Open Food Facts request, as their API terms ask. Use a real
// contact address so they can reach you if the client misbehaves.
OPEN_FOOD_FACTS_USER_AGENT = MealNotes/0.1 (personal prototype; contact@example.invalid)
